=== Psalm 36
--- Psalm 36 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 36
sub: The wicked and the fountain of life
refs: Psalm 36:1

--- The workers of iniquity fallen
kicker: Psalm 36 · A bitter end
big: Psalm 36:12 | mark: There are the workers of iniquity fallen
p: A short song ruminating on the wicked, **Psalm 36** turns this doctrine into prayer for the children of God, singing His praise.
p: The workers of iniquity come to a bitter end. They are cast down and fallen. Hell will be their home.

--- I never knew you
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 36:12
scripture: Matthew 7:22-23 | mark: I never knew you: depart from me, ye that work iniquity
p: They trust themselves, their "wonderful works," and with their lips they may claim the Lord, but their heart is far from Him.

--- No fear of God before his eyes
kicker: Psalm 36 · Pride
scripture: Psalm 36:1-2 | mark: there is no fear of God before his eyes; he flattereth himself in his own eyes
p: Their pride comes as a snare. They do not fear God, and it is evidenced by the boldness of their sin. They lie to themselves, flattering their deeds to justify them in their own eyes until their sin is found hateful, that is, to be despised.

--- He abhorreth not evil
kicker: Psalm 36 · Mischief upon his bed
scripture: Psalm 36:3-4 | mark: he abhorreth not evil
p: They lie to themselves, are haters of truth. They reject wisdom and well doing, and rather seek mischief and evil for others. They go out of their way to treat others spitefully, while not hating evil as we are commanded to.
big: Romans 1:32 | mark: have pleasure in them that do them
p: We are commanded to hate evil and cleave to what is good. They take pleasure in iniquity, even in seeing other wicked men committing it.

--- Like the great mountains
kicker: Psalm 36 · Thy mercy
scripture: Psalm 36:5-6 | mark: Thy mercy, O LORD, is in the heavens; Thy righteousness is like the great mountains
p: But for them that fear God, His mercy extends forever, His faithfulness spans the heavens. His righteousness stands like great mountains and His judgments as the great deep of oceans.

--- The fountain of life
kicker: Psalm 36 · The river of thy pleasures
scripture: Psalm 36:7-9 | mark: under the shadow of thy wings; For with thee is the fountain of life: in thy light shall we see light
p: We sing praise to His lovingkindness and seek the protection of being under His wing. His provision for us is the *fatness of* His *house*, being in Christ makes us to drink of the living waters, the fountain of life, the river of His pleasure, constantly flowing to those who love God.

--- The true light
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 36:9
p: He is the true light, and in it we see Jesus.
scripture: John 1:4-5 | mark: the life was the light of men

--- The upright in heart
kicker: Psalm 36 · Thy lovingkindness
scripture: Psalm 36:10-11 | mark: thy righteousness to the upright in heart
p: The contrast between the wicked and righteous is the fear of the Lord. The love of truth and abhorring evil. The prideful versus the humility of falling before God, and seeking His lovingkindness and protection, and receiving His imputed righteousness upon the upright of heart. Amen

=== Psalm 37
--- Psalm 37 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 37
sub: Fret not thyself • Trust in the LORD, and do good
refs: Psalm 37:1

--- Fret not thyself
kicker: Psalm 37 · Evildoers
big: Psalm 37:1 | mark: Fret not thyself because of evildoers
p: **Psalm 37** continues the theme of the wicked compared to the upright, but the tone here shifts away from the plea to be delivered from present and ready evil, to triumphant declaration of the mercy of God towards His saints. The blessed hope and the truth of judgment as a comfort for the believer.

--- Be careful for nothing
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 37:1
p: Paul writes in **Philippians 4:6** *Be careful for nothing*... We are commanded not to fear and anxiety over potential persecution is a sin. We are told here to *fret not because of evildoers*.
big: Philippians 4:6 | mark: Be careful for nothing
p: The courage we are expected to show as Christians isn't based on our own efforts or strength, it is based on the power of our God. And consider the next stanza of the opening verse, *neither be thou envious against the workers of iniquity.*

--- The meek shall inherit the earth
kicker: Psalm 37 · Cut off
scripture: Psalm 37:9-11 | mark: the meek shall inherit the earth
p: We will see that they have their rewards, our inheritance is to be preserved in this life from hunger and taken care of with our basic needs. The meek shall inherit the earth, while the workers of iniquity shall be destroyed and their seed cut off.
p: We may understand that evil loses in the end, and we have already overcome the world. Faith in this truth should lift us up.

--- Gratitude and anxiety
kicker: Application • Fret not
p: But more, these two concepts go together in a physiological truth. God has built us in a way that makes our gratitude for what we have the source for being less anxious.
p: This isn't just a "Hey, don't worry and don't envy the wicked, it's gonna work out" platitude.
p: Science shows that gratitude, being satisfied and thankful for what you have decreases activity in the amygdala, which is the part of the brain that triggers fear, panic, and stress. It moves our bodies away from the fearful "fight-or-flight" mode and into the "rest-and-digest" mode.

--- Truly grateful
kicker: Application • Fret not
p: It is impossible for those that are truly grateful to God for their station in life, their economic conditions, the blessings He has given us, and the protection of His mighty hand, to fret. To feel anxiety. To fear.
p: Practicing thankfulness boosts dopamine and serotonin, which naturally lower stress hormones like cortisol.
p: Our brain struggles to focus on threat-detection (anxiety) and present-moment safety (gratitude) at the same time.

--- With thanksgiving
kicker: Psalm 37 · Rest in the Lord
scripture: Psalm 37:7-8 | mark: Rest in the LORD, and wait patiently for him; fret not thyself
p: **Philippians 4:6** continues, ...*but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God.*
p: Science once again catches up with the truth God has given us in His word.
p: And so, when we are commanded to not be envious of evildoers, and given reasons to be thankful, God is giving us the means to not fret.
p: Gratitude for all He does, being satisfied and knowing how to be abased and abound, naturally makes us less anxious. To fear not.

--- Dwell in the land
kicker: Psalm 37 · Trust in the Lord, and do good
scripture: Psalm 37:2-3 | mark: Trust in the LORD, and do good; so shalt thou dwell in the land
p: Continuing in **Psalm 37**, God promises that the wicked shall be cut down like grass, while those that trust in the Lord and do good shall dwell in the land and be fed.
p: This is a crucial detail in the Christian life, and the fate of our families and nation. The conditional understanding, that those who trust in the Lord, His people, the children of God by faith in Christ Jesus, are given the opportunity to have life, in abundance, through obedience to the commandments of God. To do good.

--- Nations are judged by Christians' works
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 37:3
p: We are given eternal life by faith in Christ Jesus alone. Trusting God. But our lives on this earth are judged by the good that we do.
p: People are judged by faith. Nations are judged by Christians' works.
big: 2 Chronicles 7:14 | mark: If my people, which are called by my name
p: It is a popular verse, quoted often from pulpits across America. And it shows that there is a solution to the chaos and depravity affecting our society, and that solution is not found in consuming political commentary or voting. It is turning towards God and away from evil, as His people.

--- The salt of the earth
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 37:3
big: Matthew 5:13 | mark: Ye are the salt of the earth
p: Salt is a preservative that keeps meat from spoiling. It not only stops the rotting, but improves the flavor and texture of it when cooked. When tried by the fire.
p: We are to be that salt, and when the Christian loses his savour, we see that we become useless, and are left to be trodden down, and rightfully so.

--- Persecuted for righteousness' sake
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 37:3
p: This is in contrast with the earlier verse;
scripture: Matthew 5:10-12 | mark: Blessed are they which are persecuted for righteousness’ sake

--- They are not the same
kicker: Application • Salt
p: We often hear Christians complain about being vexed by the filthy conversation of the wicked, and who treat the inevitable treading down caused by our lack of commitment and separation, as persecution for righteousness sake. They are not the same.
p: In fact, many Christians live as the world, not doing good that they may dwell in the land, and act surprised when the wicked prosper and our nation is flooded with strangers, bringing their idolatry and paganism with them.

--- We are the cause
kicker: Application • Salt
p: They act as though they are being persecuted by politicians and corrupt leaders, while in reality we are the cause.
p: We become fearful and resentful, fretting about evildoers and being envious of the workers of iniquity.
p: We ignore God's prescriptions for what ails us, and so we forsake the promises of safety and provision He has given us.

--- The desires of thine heart
kicker: Psalm 37 · Delight thyself in the Lord
scripture: Psalm 37:4-5 | mark: he shall give thee the desires of thine heart
p: God promises those that trust in Him and do good will be given the desires of our hearts.
scripture: Jeremiah 17:9-10 | mark: The heart is deceitful above all things

--- Our will becomes His
kicker: Psalm 37 · Delight thyself in the Lord
p: When we are right with God, and do according to His ways, our heart's desires are His. They are upright and good, because our will becomes His when in all our ways we acknowledge Him, and allow the Lord to direct our paths.
p: He tries the reins, and sees our good works and rewards us openly. We consider the poor and broken-hearted. We take up the cause of the fatherless and widows. We spread the good news of eternal life in Christ Jesus. We live lives separated unto the gospel, and consider the profit of others above our own. Our desires become for the good of others.

--- My prayer shall be for others
kicker: Application • Others
quote: Lord help me to live from day to day / In such a self-forgetful way, / That even when I kneel to pray / My prayer shall be for others. | Others
p: This hymn encapsulates the concept.
big: James 1:27 | mark: Pure religion and undefiled
p: When we consider others, when we love our neighbors and even our enemies, when we practice pure religion, the outward works of our faith, it should be for others.
p: And our keeping ourselves from the filth of the world is part of that, that God will see our good works, and give us our desires, and make all things come to pass.

--- As the light
kicker: Psalm 37 · Thy righteousness as the light
big: Psalm 37:6
p: We are the light of the world. Our purpose is to magnify the light within us that others, yes others, see it and glorify God.
big: Matthew 5:16 | mark: Let your light so shine before men
p: By trusting the Lord and doing good, we preserve our nation, we get to dwell in the land, we are fed, and we see our hearts turn from desiring our own wicked lusts, to seeking good for others.

